# Secrets — never commit.
#
# Patterns are deliberately narrow. A broad glob such as `*secret*` also
# excludes source files that merely discuss secrets — it silently dropped
# tests/security/test_secret_hygiene.py from the first commit of this
# repository, which would have left CI running without the tests that prove
# secrets stay out of logs.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.keystore
secrets.json
secrets.yaml
secrets.yml
credentials.json
.credentials

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
*.egg-info/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
coverage.xml
htmlcov/
requirements-audit.txt

# Data — raw captures and datasets never enter git
data/
raw/
*.parquet
*.duckdb
research/datasets/*
!research/datasets/.gitkeep
research/notebooks/.ipynb_checkpoints/

# Evidence artifacts are generated. The acceptance-attempt log is not: it
# records verification attempts and their blockers, which must survive in
# version control rather than in a workflow artifact that expires.
docs/evidence/build-0.1/*
!docs/evidence/build-0.1/README.md
!docs/evidence/build-0.1/acceptance-attempts.md
# Recording reports are the exception to the exception. They are generated,
# but the run they describe is gone by the time anyone reads them, so they are
# not regenerable in the sense the rule above assumes - and they are how the
# recording host tells everyone else what it saw.
!docs/evidence/build-0.1/recordings/
# Strategy evaluations, for the same reason and a second one. They are the
# record of what was tested against what data, and Phase 4's overfitting
# discipline only works if that record exists: a holdout is spent once, and
# "which rules had already been tried" is not a question anyone should have to
# answer from memory.
!docs/evidence/build-0.1/evaluations/

# Node
node_modules/
.next/
dist/

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/
